  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nNo precip is expected today (THU) through at least FRI evening,\noutside of a very low chance (<10%) for a quick pop-up \nthunderstorm in our far western CWA. \n\nTomorrow (FRI), temps will be in the 80s F to low 90s, but heat\nindices will not a concern. A few isolated thunderstorms will \nbe possible for our southwestern CWA during the early evening as\na surface low develops in SD and an approaching associated \nfront stalls over west-central KS. This front will stall beneath\nsubtle mid-level diffluence / jet forcing. However, confidence \nin these storms developing remains low as the best jet streak / \nadvective forcing aloft looks to remain off to the east, and \n700-hPa temperatures are expected to hold around 10 C. Overall, \n12z HREF probabilities suggest 30 - 50% chances for these storms\nto develop. If storms do develop, however, severe hazards will \nbe possible with pockets of severe winds and hail owing to steep\nlow-level lapse rates beneath thicker CAPE profiles in the hail\ngrowth zone. Additionally, southerly sustained winds of 15 - 20\nmph with gusts up to 30 mph are possible for the northern and \ncentral portions of our CWA, particularly in the during peak \nheating. \n\nOvernight FRI into SAT, a few more storms are anticipated to \nwork across our north-central CWA counties as the stalled front \nbegins to advance east with its parent shortwave. SPC currently \nhas this area on the edge of their SLGT risk zone, with wind and\nhail as hazards. Marginally-severe hail looks the likeliest as \nstorms are expected to stay elevated on the cool side of the \npassing front, which will likely help limit winds aloft from \nmixing down to the surface. Non- thunderstorm gusts early SAT \nmorning can reach 25 mph on the backside of the advancing front \nfor our western and northern counties, with lows bottoming out \nin the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\nIsolated to scattered showers will linger for eastern counties \non SAT through the late morning and afternoon hours. The \nprevious night's cold front (potentially augmented by stalled \noutflow) will come to rest just southeast of our KS counties. \nStorms may initiate along this boundary in the evening and move \nalong / north of this boundary, as indicated by most members of \nthe 12z REFS. Given PWATs of 1.6 - 1.8 in on SAT evening, \nlocalized flooding for our far southeastern CWA will need to be \nmonitored, but no major impacts are anticipated.\n\nSunday will be serve as a transition day between two troughs. \nScattered showers and thunderstorms are possible, but severe \nprobabilities look low. An isothermal layer up to near 700 mb \nowing to ridging between the troughs will likely keep most of \nthis precip non-storm related, where storms would be reserved to\nlocalized pockets of strong mesoscale ascent. SUN will also be \nbreezy, with gusts in the 25 - 30 mph range from late morning \nlasting through SUN and into MON. \n\nMonday is expected to return severe chances to our CWA as the \nnext trough arrives in the evening hours with 35 - 45 kts of \nright- entrance flow at 500 mb per the 12z GEFS. The 06z EPS \nalso suggests that a potent 850-mb LLJ will be in place late MON\nnight with the nose placed over east-central NE. SPC currently \nholds us in their Day 5 15% outlook. Outside of storms, MON will\nbe windier with gusts up to 35 mph. Lows Tuesday morning behind\nthe cold front will dip to the upper 40s F for our far \nnorthwestern CWA, and broader 50s F for most.",
